# Line-ending policy — load-bearing, not cosmetic.
#
# `plugins/builtin/*/plugin.runtime-binding.json` records a SHA-256 of each
# descriptor file's *raw bytes*, and `load_verified_binding` recomputes it at
# runtime to decide whether a plugin is still "verified":
#
#     protocol_hash = _sha256(protocol_path)
#     if binding["protocol"]["sha256"] != protocol_hash:
#         raise PluginRuntimeError("protocol binding does not match its verified descriptor")
#
# With `core.autocrlf=true` a Windows checkout produced CRLF bytes while a Linux
# checkout produced LF, so the *same commit* verified on Windows and failed
# everywhere else — 22 tests failed in CI with exactly that error.  Pinning LF
# makes the bytes a file hashes identical on every platform, so the recorded
# hashes stay true.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native scripting keeps CRLF: cmd.exe is unreliable with LF-only batch
# files, and nothing hashes these, so there is no cost to leaving them alone.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# The high-difficulty case's evidence snapshot is hash-addressed: the audit
# report, `_tools/source_integrity.json`, the render fixtures and the flow-DAG
# page all cite the SHA-256 of its *raw bytes*.  Its generator
# (`_tools/audit_verify_hard.py`) writes through a plain text handle, so on
# Windows it emits CRLF — and with `core.autocrlf=true` the commit stored LF
# instead, which made the cited hash true locally and false in CI.
#
# `-text -eol` means "convert in neither direction": the bytes that were hashed
# are the bytes every platform checks out, so the citation holds without having
# to rewrite the half-dozen artefacts that quote it.  (`-text` alone is not
# enough — the `* text=auto eol=lf` rule above still sets `eol`.)
审计项目案例报告效果展示/黔岭酒业2025年度财务报表审计_实验组_高难度/_tools/audit_evidence.json -text -eol

# Never attempt text normalisation on binaries.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.db   binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.pyd  binary
